Verdict: PASS — I've written it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

I tested the real binary in both directions, run with --yes from /tmp/p245e:
• With `YOYO_BLOCK_OUTSIDE_WRITES=1`: write_file to /tmp/p245e_probe.txt was refused, and the file was never created. The model got the refusal and told the user how to allow the write.
• Without the variable: the same write went through and the file contained probe, so users who don't set it see no change.

What the diff does against the task:
• Opt-in and hard: it's off unless the variable is exactly 1, it's read once per run, and it refuses without asking even under --yes.
• Same anchor as the read fence: it uses the launch directory recorded at startup (main.rs:1260), which /cd never moves. Both fences now share one is_inside check, and the read fence's old no-anchor behaviour is unchanged.
• Path handling:
  • A relative path resolves against the current directory at call time.
  • A target that doesn't exist yet is checked through its nearest existing parent.
  • A symlink inside the project that points outside is refused.
  • A .. or a dangling symlink in the not-yet-existing part is refused, because yoyo can't tell where it leads.
  • --allow-dir counts as consent, as it does for reads.
• Scope: the doc comment and the docs both say that bash and rename_symbol are not covered, and why.
• Refusal type: the refusal is classed the same way as the read fence's, so the recovery-hint wrapper won't treat it as a flaky tool.
• Tests: they cover flag off, inside, a new nested path, a symlink, a .. escape, and the anchor staying put when the cwd moves. A wiring test goes through both wrappers, maybe_guard for the main agent and maybe_guard_arc for sub-agents.
• Nothing left unused: every new definition is read somewhere in this diff.
• Size gate: tool_wrappers.rs only changed one comment line at each call site, so its line count didn't move and tests/module_size.rs didn't need updating.
• Docs: docs/src/configuration/permissions.md has a new section next to the read fence.

